---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Contents from "../components/Contents.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Chapter from "../components/chapter/Chapter.astro";
import EnergyTray from "../components/chapter/EnergyTray.tsx";
import FigureTray from "../components/chapter/FigureTray.tsx";
import PartName from "../components/chapter/PartName.astro";
import Step from "../components/chapter/Step.astro";
import Movement from "../components/drawing/Movement.astro";
import { dialSvg } from "../movement/svg/dial.ts";
import { BALANCE_START, closeUpSvg, ESCAPEMENT_HOLDS, poseAt, readoutsAt } from "../movement/svg/escapement.ts";

// One long page: the hero, the contents band, then the chapters in order; chapter 7 is the footer. Chapters 1 to 4
// are built on the chapter engine (components/chapter); the others are still their heads, which their briefs fill in.
const chapters = [
  { id: "together", head: "5. Together, apart and back again" },
  { id: "bench", head: "6. The bench, Tavi’s tools" },
];

// Chapter 1's figure opens half apart, as its first step stands; the tray's Take apart starts there too.
const energyOpening = { e: 0.55 };
// Chapter 2 lifts nothing: its steps walk down the train, then turn the figure over to the dial.
const wheelsPose = { e: 0 };
// Both of chapter 2's faces frame the same stretch of the movement, mirrored about the centre arbor, so turning the
// figure over keeps every part where it was.
const wheelsFrame = "-245 -205 490 410";

// Chapters 3 and 4 are drawn by the clock (lib/clockFigure.ts): their figures lift nothing. Chapter 3's steps each hold
// one phase of a beat, so its drawing is built at step 1's moment, locked; chapter 4 runs from the start.
const still = { e: 0 };
const escapementStart = ESCAPEMENT_HOLDS["1"] ?? 0;
const escapementReadouts = readoutsAt(poseAt(escapementStart));
const balanceReadouts = readoutsAt(poseAt(BALANCE_START));
---

<Base>
  <Header />
  <main id="top">
    <Hero />
    <Contents />

    <Chapter
      id="energy"
      n={1}
      head="1. Energy, wound into a spring"
      caption="The barrel, opened: the mainspring coiled inside, the bridge, ratchet wheel and crown wheel lifted above it."
      opening={energyOpening}
      next={{ href: "#wheels", head: "2. Wheels, hours into seconds" }}
    >
      <Step n={1} pose={energyOpening}>
        Everything here runs on one coiled spring, the <PartName part="mainspring">mainspring</PartName>. It lives
        inside the <PartName part="barrel">barrel</PartName>, the toothed drum beside the crown.
        <Fragment slot="hint">
          Slide <span class="ctl">Take apart</span> to see inside.
        </Fragment>
      </Step>
      <Step n={2} pose={{ e: 0.55 }}>
        Turning the <PartName part="crown">crown</PartName> turns the <PartName part="ratchet">ratchet wheel</PartName>,
        and the spring coils tighter round its <PartName part="arbor">arbor</PartName>. The
        <PartName part="click">click</PartName> stops it slipping back.
        <Fragment slot="hint">
          Drag <span class="ctl">Wind</span> to coil it.
        </Fragment>
      </Step>
      <Step n={3} pose={{ e: 0.2 }}>
        The spring presses on the barrel wall, so the barrel turns: in this movement, once every six hours. Its
        <span class="fig">72</span> teeth drive the <PartName part="centre">centre wheel</PartName>.
      </Step>
      <Step n={4} pose={{ e: 0 }}>
        Seven turns of the barrel hold about <span class="fig">41</span> hours. Left free, the spring would spend them
        in seconds. Chapter 3 shows what holds it back.
      </Step>
      <Movement
        slot="figure"
        pose="chapter"
        id="energy-movement"
        viewBox="-255 -250 420 345"
        aria-label="Diagram of the barrel, opened: the mainspring coiled round its arbor inside the toothed drum, with the barrel bridge, ratchet wheel, crown wheel and click lifted above it."
      />
      <EnergyTray slot="tray" client:visible figure="energy-figure" opening={energyOpening} />
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter
      id="wheels"
      n={2}
      head="2. Wheels, hours into seconds"
      caption="The going train from the back with its bridges off: the barrel, the centre, third and fourth wheels and the escape wheel, each mesh's ratio beside it. Turned over in step 5, the dial side: the hands at 10:10:30 and the small seconds over the fourth wheel."
      opening={wheelsPose}
      next={{ href: "#escapement", head: "3. Escapement, let out tooth by tooth" }}
    >
      <Step n={1} pose={wheelsPose}>
        The <PartName part="barrel">barrel</PartName>’s <span class="fig">72</span> teeth drive the
        <PartName part="centre">centre wheel</PartName>’s pinion of <span class="fig">12</span> leaves: six to one. So
        the barrel turns once in six hours.
      </Step>
      <Step n={2} pose={wheelsPose}>
        The <PartName part="centre">centre wheel</PartName> turns once an hour; its arbor comes through to the dial and
        carries the minute hand. Its <span class="fig">80</span> teeth turn the
        <PartName part="third">third wheel</PartName>’s <span class="fig">10</span> leaves: eight turns an hour.
      </Step>
      <Step n={3} pose={wheelsPose}>
        <PartName part="third">Third wheel</PartName> to fourth: <span class="fig">75</span> teeth on
        <span class="fig">10</span> leaves. Eight turns an hour become sixty, so the
        <PartName part="fourth">fourth wheel</PartName> turns once a minute, the speed of a seconds hand.
      </Step>
      <Step n={4} pose={wheelsPose}>
        Fourth to escape: <span class="fig">84</span> teeth on <span class="fig">7</span> leaves, twelve to one. The
        <PartName part="escape">escape wheel</PartName> turns twelve times a minute. Chapter 3 shows what it does.
      </Step>
      <Step n={5} pose={wheelsPose}>
        Turned over, the dial side. A cannon pinion of <span class="fig">10</span> rides the centre arbor and drives a
        minute wheel of <span class="fig">30</span>, whose pinion of <span class="fig">8</span> drives an hour wheel of
        <span class="fig">32</span>: twelve to one. So the <PartName part="hour-hand">hour hand</PartName> goes round
        twice a day under the <PartName part="minute-hand">minute hand</PartName>, and the
        <PartName part="seconds-hand">small seconds</PartName> rides the fourth wheel. The hands are blued steel, in the
        shape Breguet designed in 1783.
      </Step>
      <div class="turnover" slot="figure">
        <Movement
          pose="train"
          id="wheels-train"
          viewBox={wheelsFrame}
          aria-label="Diagram of the going train from the back, its bridges off: the barrel drives the centre wheel (72 / 12), the centre wheel the third (80 / 10), the third the fourth (75 / 10), and the fourth the escape wheel (84 / 7)."
        />
        <Fragment
          set:html={dialSvg({
            id: "wheels-dial",
            viewBox: wheelsFrame,
            label:
              "Diagram of the dial side: a minute track, applied indices, blued Breguet hour and minute hands at 10:10, and the small seconds at about eight o’clock, over the fourth wheel.",
          })}
        />
      </div>
      <FigureTray slot="tray" client:visible figure="wheels-figure" />
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter
      id="escapement"
      n={3}
      head="3. Escapement, let out tooth by tooth"
      caption="A close-up of the escapement from the back, the bridges off and the balance cut away round its staff: the escape wheel, the pallet fork with its two ruby stones between two banking pins, and the roller with its ruby impulse pin in the fork’s notch."
      opening={still}
      speed={10}
      next={{ href: "#balance", head: "4. Balance, six swings a second" }}
    >
      <Step n={1} pose={still}>
        This is what holds the spring back. A tooth of the <PartName part="escape">escape wheel</PartName> rests on one
        of the two <PartName part="stones">pallet stones</PartName>, and the whole train waits on it. The
        <PartName part="pallet">pallet fork</PartName> that carries the stones lies against one of its banking pins.
      </Step>
      <Step n={2} pose={still}>
        The <PartName part="balance">balance</PartName> swings back through the middle. Its
        <PartName part="impulse-pin">impulse pin</PartName>, set in the <PartName part="roller">roller</PartName> on its
        staff, catches the fork’s notch and turns the fork. The stone slides off the tooth, and the wheel even backs up
        a hair: the recoil.
      </Step>
      <Step n={3} pose={still}>
        Now the tooth pushes on the stone’s slanted end, its impulse face. The push runs through the fork to the
        <PartName part="impulse-pin">impulse pin</PartName>, and the balance gets its kick for the next swing. Here the
        wheel turns about ten degrees giving it.
      </Step>
      <Step n={4} pose={still}>
        The tooth slips off the stone’s far corner and the wheel jumps free, the drop, until the other stone catches the
        next tooth. Locked again, half a tooth on, and the fork rests against its other banking pin.
      </Step>
      <Step n={5} pose={still}>
        Twelve degrees a beat: two beats let one of the <PartName part="escape">escape wheel</PartName>’s
        <span class="fig">15</span> teeth pass, so it turns once in <span class="fig">30</span> beats, five seconds.
        Thomas Mudge made the first lever escapement in England around 1755; nearly every mechanical watch made since
        1900 has one.
      </Step>
      <Fragment
        slot="figure"
        set:html={closeUpSvg({
          pose: "escapement",
          id: "escapement-drawing",
          viewBox: "-42 54 141 105",
          label:
            "Diagram of the escapement close up: the escape wheel’s fifteen teeth, the pallet fork with two ruby pallet stones, one holding a tooth, the banking pins either side of the fork, and the roller with its impulse pin.",
          t: escapementStart,
        })}
      />
      <FigureTray slot="tray" client:visible figure="escapement-figure" speed={10} />
      <p slot="tray" class="readout">
        <span>Beats</span>
        <span class="fig" data-readout="beats">
          {escapementReadouts.beats}
        </span>
      </p>
      <p slot="tray" class="readout">
        <span>Escape wheel</span>
        <span class="fig" data-readout="escape">
          {escapementReadouts.escape}
        </span>
      </p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter
      id="balance"
      n={4}
      head="4. Balance, six swings a second"
      caption="The balance close up from the back, its cock off: the rim with its three arms and four timing screws, the hairspring coiled on top from the collet out to its stud, the roller and impulse pin under it, the pallet fork beside it, and the lift window drawn as a thin arc towards the fork."
      opening={still}
      next={{ href: "#together", head: "5. Together, apart and back again" }}
    >
      <Step n={1} pose={still}>
        The <PartName part="balance">balance</PartName> is the part that keeps the time. It swings one way until the
        <PartName part="hairspring">hairspring</PartName> coiled on its staff pulls it back, then the other way until
        the spring pushes it back again.
      </Step>
      <Step n={2} pose={still}>
        Each swing is one beat: six a second, <span class="fig">21,600</span> an hour, three times out and back. That is
        3 Hz, the rate of a Seiko NH35.
      </Step>
      <Step n={3} pose={still}>
        As it swings, the <PartName part="hairspring">hairspring</PartName> breathes: its coils open out as the balance
        unwinds it and close in as it winds it back. At 1/10 you can watch them.
      </Step>
      <Step n={4} pose={still}>
        The <PartName part="pallet">pallet fork</PartName> can push only while the
        <PartName part="impulse-pin">impulse pin</PartName> crosses the lift window, here
        <span class="fig">50</span> degrees in the middle of a swing of <span class="fig">200</span> each way. The rest
        of the time the balance swings free, so its rate is set by the balance and its spring, not by the push.
      </Step>
      <Step n={5} pose={still}>
        Christiaan Huygens put a spiral spring on a watch balance in 1675, and watches came to keep time within about
        ten minutes a day. A new NH35 is made to keep within <span class="fig">−20</span> and
        <span class="fig">+40</span> seconds a day.
      </Step>
      <Fragment
        slot="figure"
        set:html={closeUpSvg({
          pose: "balance",
          id: "balance-drawing",
          viewBox: "-62 76 150 112",
          label:
            "Diagram of the balance close up: the rim with three arms and four timing screws, the hairspring coiled on top, the roller and impulse pin under it, and a thin arc marking the lift window towards the pallet fork.",
          t: BALANCE_START,
        })}
      />
      <FigureTray slot="tray" client:visible figure="balance-figure" />
      <p slot="tray" class="readout">
        <span>Amplitude</span>
        <span class="fig" data-readout="amplitude">
          {balanceReadouts.amplitude}
        </span>
      </p>
      <p slot="tray" class="readout">
        <span>Balance</span>
        <span class="fig" data-readout="balance">
          {balanceReadouts.balance}
        </span>
      </p>
    </Chapter>

    {chapters.map((chapter) => (
      <section class="chapter" id={chapter.id} aria-labelledby={`${chapter.id}-head`}>
        <div class="wrap">
          <div class="chapter-head">
            <h2 id={`${chapter.id}-head`}>{chapter.head}</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<script>
  import { startClockFigure } from "../lib/clockFigure";
  import { BALANCE_START, ESCAPEMENT_HOLDS } from "../movement/svg/escapement";

  startClockFigure("escapement", { holds: ESCAPEMENT_HOLDS, start: ESCAPEMENT_HOLDS["1"] ?? 0 });
  startClockFigure("balance", { start: BALANCE_START });
</script>
